WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.940
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:06.870 --> 00:00:12.530
SLAWEK WALKOWSKI: Lightweight,
fast, highly available.

00:00:12.530 --> 00:00:16.520
This is how serverless
is often described.

00:00:16.520 --> 00:00:21.400
And it's all true, but making
your solution reliable--

00:00:21.400 --> 00:00:22.720
and performance--

00:00:22.720 --> 00:00:25.390
depends also on you.

00:00:25.390 --> 00:00:27.010
Hi, I'm Slawek.

00:00:27.010 --> 00:00:30.840
I work as a software engineer
in the Cloud Functions team

00:00:30.840 --> 00:00:32.290
at Google.

00:00:32.290 --> 00:00:35.080
And together with
my colleague Marek,

00:00:35.080 --> 00:00:40.990
we will talk about ways to
build solid production systems

00:00:40.990 --> 00:00:43.990
with Cloud Functions.

00:00:43.990 --> 00:00:47.650
Now, Cloud Functions is
an event-driven serverless

00:00:47.650 --> 00:00:49.860
computer platform.

00:00:49.860 --> 00:00:53.520
Someone or something
generates an event,

00:00:53.520 --> 00:00:59.340
and your code, your function,
is executed as a result of it.

00:00:59.340 --> 00:01:01.140
This is pretty much
the simplest way

00:01:01.140 --> 00:01:03.540
to run your code in the cloud.

00:01:03.540 --> 00:01:06.780
There are no servers to be
provisioned, managed, patched,

00:01:06.780 --> 00:01:08.460
or updated.

00:01:08.460 --> 00:01:11.400
You pay only while
your code runs.

00:01:11.400 --> 00:01:15.640
You can connect and extend
multiple cloud services.

00:01:15.640 --> 00:01:18.120
And finally, Cloud
Functions automatically

00:01:18.120 --> 00:01:21.960
scale, are highly available,
and fault tolerant.

00:01:21.960 --> 00:01:25.680
But does it mean that all
your potential problems

00:01:25.680 --> 00:01:28.170
with availability
and performance

00:01:28.170 --> 00:01:31.140
are automatically resolved?

00:01:31.140 --> 00:01:33.480
The answer is not
completely, as you can still

00:01:33.480 --> 00:01:38.520
apply multiple best practices
to build a robust system.

00:01:38.520 --> 00:01:42.150
And we're going to talk
about them in this talk.

00:01:42.150 --> 00:01:45.930
First we will cover
reliability improvements,

00:01:45.930 --> 00:01:49.500
then we move on to
performance optimizations,

00:01:49.500 --> 00:01:52.965
and additionally, we will
also discuss observability,

00:01:52.965 --> 00:01:54.840
which is important when
you want to know what

00:01:54.840 --> 00:01:58.300
is going on with your system.

00:01:58.300 --> 00:02:01.330
Let's start with talking
over reliability improvements

00:02:01.330 --> 00:02:03.820
through making
your Cloud Function

00:02:03.820 --> 00:02:07.690
retryable and idempotent.

00:02:07.690 --> 00:02:11.890
We first need to realize
that in distributed systems

00:02:11.890 --> 00:02:14.050
failures happen.

00:02:14.050 --> 00:02:16.860
In fact, they are an inherent
part of such systems,

00:02:16.860 --> 00:02:22.210
as it's practically impossible
to build a solid production

00:02:22.210 --> 00:02:26.580
system or any complex system
in which errors do not

00:02:26.580 --> 00:02:28.780
occur at all.

00:02:28.780 --> 00:02:33.480
The only way we
can handle them is

00:02:33.480 --> 00:02:36.420
to make sure that we handle
the errors gracefully,

00:02:36.420 --> 00:02:39.510
and cloud platform actually
provided significant help

00:02:39.510 --> 00:02:41.760
in handling such errors.

00:02:41.760 --> 00:02:45.000
It is actually one of
the big benefits of using

00:02:45.000 --> 00:02:47.350
cloud and server solutions.

00:02:47.350 --> 00:02:49.440
However, your
software still needs

00:02:49.440 --> 00:02:53.430
to be prepared for
error scenarios.

00:02:53.430 --> 00:02:56.730
If we think about who
ensures reliability

00:02:56.730 --> 00:03:00.420
of your software
running in the cloud,

00:03:00.420 --> 00:03:02.460
we realize that
reliability must be

00:03:02.460 --> 00:03:07.230
a shared goal across the cloud
platform and your solution.

00:03:07.230 --> 00:03:10.470
When you use a
Google Cloud Service,

00:03:10.470 --> 00:03:13.260
Google takes care
of its reliability.

00:03:13.260 --> 00:03:17.070
For example, when you
call a cloud Vision API,

00:03:17.070 --> 00:03:19.890
Google makes sure that the
service is available and can

00:03:19.890 --> 00:03:21.970
handle your request.

00:03:21.970 --> 00:03:25.450
When you upload a
file to cloud storage,

00:03:25.450 --> 00:03:29.200
Google makes sure it's
persistent using replication

00:03:29.200 --> 00:03:32.070
under the hood.

00:03:32.070 --> 00:03:35.190
When you create your
service in the cloud,

00:03:35.190 --> 00:03:38.190
Google still helps you
build a robust system,

00:03:38.190 --> 00:03:41.910
but you are in control of
its overall reliability.

00:03:41.910 --> 00:03:46.630
For example, your function
may fail if it includes a bug.

00:03:46.630 --> 00:03:50.460
A call to your other
service may also

00:03:50.460 --> 00:03:55.020
fail if that service returns
an error or is flaky.

00:03:57.630 --> 00:04:02.320
How to ensure reliability
of your Cloud Function.

00:04:02.320 --> 00:04:06.430
As we already know, functions
are good for processing events.

00:04:06.430 --> 00:04:09.250
In particular,
they're a great tool

00:04:09.250 --> 00:04:12.290
when connecting different
services, for example,

00:04:12.290 --> 00:04:13.900
when processing
the incoming data

00:04:13.900 --> 00:04:17.610
and uploading it to
a storage system.

00:04:17.610 --> 00:04:21.190
In the success scenario,
everything works.

00:04:21.190 --> 00:04:23.740
The data is processed and
then persisted, for example,

00:04:23.740 --> 00:04:26.230
in a database.

00:04:26.230 --> 00:04:30.010
But what if an error
occurs in this process?

00:04:30.010 --> 00:04:32.830
Your function may
run out of memory.

00:04:32.830 --> 00:04:36.390
A network connectivity
issue may happen.

00:04:36.390 --> 00:04:39.105
You may get an error
response from the service

00:04:39.105 --> 00:04:41.260
on which you depend.

00:04:41.260 --> 00:04:45.880
All these issues may
potentially result in data loss

00:04:45.880 --> 00:04:50.730
if the data doesn't make
it to its destination.

00:04:50.730 --> 00:04:53.760
How to handle
problems like this.

00:04:53.760 --> 00:04:57.480
Retries are an
important strategy here.

00:04:57.480 --> 00:04:59.670
In a distributed
system, it is normal

00:04:59.670 --> 00:05:03.240
that the services on which
your function depends

00:05:03.240 --> 00:05:06.300
are not always 100% available.

00:05:06.300 --> 00:05:11.330
As a result, your function
may fail occasionally.

00:05:11.330 --> 00:05:14.840
The simplest way to handle
failures in serverless

00:05:14.840 --> 00:05:17.700
is to retry function executions.

00:05:17.700 --> 00:05:21.290
However, you should
not force your users

00:05:21.290 --> 00:05:24.320
to retry requests
through your system,

00:05:24.320 --> 00:05:29.420
but instead you should configure
your system to perform retries

00:05:29.420 --> 00:05:30.860
automatically when needed.

00:05:33.460 --> 00:05:36.980
Cloud Functions come in
two distinct variants.

00:05:36.980 --> 00:05:41.560
HTTP functions are invoked
by standard HTTP requests,

00:05:41.560 --> 00:05:46.440
which wait for HTTP
response from the function.

00:05:46.440 --> 00:05:48.870
Background functions
are involved

00:05:48.870 --> 00:05:53.330
by events from the clouds, like
messages from a cloud pub/sub

00:05:53.330 --> 00:05:58.740
topic, or changes in a
Cloud Storage bucket.

00:05:58.740 --> 00:06:04.380
All functions are normally
invoked once for an event,

00:06:04.380 --> 00:06:06.270
unless an error occurs.

00:06:06.270 --> 00:06:09.770
And here is where the two
variants of Cloud Functions

00:06:09.770 --> 00:06:13.340
offer different
execution guarantees.

00:06:13.340 --> 00:06:16.915
HTTP functions are executed
at the most once due

00:06:16.915 --> 00:06:19.250
to their synchronous nature.

00:06:19.250 --> 00:06:22.610
This means that the calls are
not retried by cloud functions,

00:06:22.610 --> 00:06:26.259
and it is the caller who
must handle any errors.

00:06:26.259 --> 00:06:27.800
And if you think
about it, the caller

00:06:27.800 --> 00:06:30.890
should handle the errors anyway,
as the request might not even

00:06:30.890 --> 00:06:34.470
reach Google's infrastructure.

00:06:34.470 --> 00:06:36.600
On the other hand,
background functions

00:06:36.600 --> 00:06:40.570
are executed at least once,
as asynchronous function

00:06:40.570 --> 00:06:43.470
executions triggered
by events can

00:06:43.470 --> 00:06:46.680
be retried by cloud functions,
as there is no caller who

00:06:46.680 --> 00:06:50.040
could do it otherwise.

00:06:50.040 --> 00:06:52.930
Let's look at some examples.

00:06:52.930 --> 00:06:57.900
This is an HTTP function
which calls another service

00:06:57.900 --> 00:07:00.670
to upload the payload
from the HTTP requests.

00:07:00.670 --> 00:07:07.380
It simply sends a post
request to that service.

00:07:07.380 --> 00:07:09.710
Easy, right?

00:07:09.710 --> 00:07:13.580
The problem is that the
call to the other service

00:07:13.580 --> 00:07:16.310
may fail for whatever
reason, for example, network

00:07:16.310 --> 00:07:20.270
connectivity issue, and this
would result in the function

00:07:20.270 --> 00:07:23.480
failure, and if the
function isn't retried then

00:07:23.480 --> 00:07:25.190
the data may be
dropped on the floor.

00:07:28.900 --> 00:07:32.140
In the case of HTTP
functions, the calls

00:07:32.140 --> 00:07:35.320
should be retried by the caller.

00:07:35.320 --> 00:07:40.030
So you can change
your caller's codes

00:07:40.030 --> 00:07:43.120
to either implement
your own retry logic,

00:07:43.120 --> 00:07:48.250
or use an existing library or
module that can perform retries

00:07:48.250 --> 00:07:52.160
for you, for example,
in this case,

00:07:52.160 --> 00:07:59.920
the promise retry module retries
the calls to Cloud Function,

00:07:59.920 --> 00:08:06.390
because we return the request
promise inside the promise

00:08:06.390 --> 00:08:08.260
retry call.

00:08:11.690 --> 00:08:13.640
This is a simple
background function.

00:08:13.640 --> 00:08:17.180
It also uploads data
to another system.

00:08:17.180 --> 00:08:19.790
This time it
extracts the contents

00:08:19.790 --> 00:08:25.310
from the Cloud Pub/Sub
message payload.

00:08:25.310 --> 00:08:29.810
Similar problem here-- if
the dependent service fails,

00:08:29.810 --> 00:08:32.690
the function will fail
because we turned the promise

00:08:32.690 --> 00:08:37.539
as the function result.

00:08:37.539 --> 00:08:39.940
In the case of
background functions

00:08:39.940 --> 00:08:42.760
retries should be configured
on function deployments,

00:08:42.760 --> 00:08:45.640
either through Cloud
SDK or Cloud Console,

00:08:45.640 --> 00:08:49.740
by enabling the retry
on failure option.

00:08:49.740 --> 00:08:55.010
This way, your function will
be retried until it succeeds.

00:08:55.010 --> 00:08:57.520
The first retry happens
almost immediately

00:08:57.520 --> 00:08:59.950
after the first
function execution.

00:08:59.950 --> 00:09:03.900
The next retry follows the
exponential backoff policy,

00:09:03.900 --> 00:09:08.320
capped at 10 seconds, and they
happen for up to seven days

00:09:08.320 --> 00:09:12.140
if your function keeps failing.

00:09:12.140 --> 00:09:15.050
However, you should be
careful with using this option

00:09:15.050 --> 00:09:19.940
because it will retry your
function on any error,

00:09:19.940 --> 00:09:25.320
also if it is caused by a bug
or some unexpected input data.

00:09:25.320 --> 00:09:28.970
So if you use this option,
you should return errors

00:09:28.970 --> 00:09:32.030
from your function only
on transient issues

00:09:32.030 --> 00:09:35.360
and preferably handle
the permanent problems

00:09:35.360 --> 00:09:37.688
inside the functions completely.

00:09:40.970 --> 00:09:44.700
So in general there are multiple
places to apply retries.

00:09:44.700 --> 00:09:48.620
As we saw, you can retry
calls to an HTTP function.

00:09:48.620 --> 00:09:53.060
You can configure retries
of the background functions.

00:09:53.060 --> 00:09:55.310
You can also retry calls
to dependent systems

00:09:55.310 --> 00:09:57.140
from inside the function.

00:09:57.140 --> 00:10:00.860
For example, by configuring the
client libraries that you use,

00:10:00.860 --> 00:10:05.000
you can search for what
options they offer,

00:10:05.000 --> 00:10:07.360
or you can write custom code.

00:10:07.360 --> 00:10:09.832
And finally, as
already mentioned,

00:10:09.832 --> 00:10:11.540
persistent errors
should be distinguished

00:10:11.540 --> 00:10:16.220
from transient failures, and
retries should be applied only

00:10:16.220 --> 00:10:17.160
on the latter ones.

00:10:19.820 --> 00:10:23.810
Let's look at a more
complex example now.

00:10:23.810 --> 00:10:26.960
Here is a function
which processes the data

00:10:26.960 --> 00:10:30.320
and writes it to two
storage systems--

00:10:30.320 --> 00:10:34.090
first to the upper one,
and then to the lower one.

00:10:36.870 --> 00:10:40.230
The problem is, as you may
expect, that any of the uploads

00:10:40.230 --> 00:10:41.790
may fail.

00:10:41.790 --> 00:10:43.500
For example, let's
assume that the upload

00:10:43.500 --> 00:10:45.505
to the second storage fails.

00:10:49.470 --> 00:10:52.290
Well, in this case,
the data would not

00:10:52.290 --> 00:10:57.000
be saved in the storage, but we
know how to handle this, right?

00:10:57.000 --> 00:10:59.010
Apply retries.

00:10:59.010 --> 00:11:03.330
But are retries always safe?

00:11:03.330 --> 00:11:07.380
For example here,
if we apply retries,

00:11:07.380 --> 00:11:09.060
the second function
execution will

00:11:09.060 --> 00:11:12.510
result in storing the data
in the second storage,

00:11:12.510 --> 00:11:15.660
assuming that the upload
succeeds this time.

00:11:15.660 --> 00:11:20.130
However, the first storage
will receive a duplicate

00:11:20.130 --> 00:11:23.700
because the upload to the
first storage will be retried,

00:11:23.700 --> 00:11:26.070
and duplicates
introduce inconsistency.

00:11:26.070 --> 00:11:28.930
The inconsistencies,
they are not good,

00:11:28.930 --> 00:11:30.300
so we don't want them.

00:11:30.300 --> 00:11:32.730
Can we do anything to
prepare the function

00:11:32.730 --> 00:11:38.760
for retry executions to avoid
issues like data duplication?

00:11:38.760 --> 00:11:41.610
The answer is yes, and
the concept of idempotency

00:11:41.610 --> 00:11:43.770
comes in handy here.

00:11:43.770 --> 00:11:46.290
A function is
considered idempotent

00:11:46.290 --> 00:11:49.380
if an event results in the
desired outcome and side

00:11:49.380 --> 00:11:52.500
effects, even if the function
is invoked multiple times

00:11:52.500 --> 00:11:54.180
for the same event.

00:11:54.180 --> 00:11:57.100
So making a function
idempotent is actually

00:11:57.100 --> 00:12:00.960
required to make
it behave correctly

00:12:00.960 --> 00:12:05.030
on retry execution attempts.

00:12:05.030 --> 00:12:08.600
Let's look at the
non-idempotent function first.

00:12:08.600 --> 00:12:12.740
This function uploads
the received contents

00:12:12.740 --> 00:12:17.630
to Firestore first, and then
it uploads the same contents

00:12:17.630 --> 00:12:23.910
to another service by
sending a post request.

00:12:23.910 --> 00:12:28.723
One potential problem
here is that if the upload

00:12:28.723 --> 00:12:34.540
to the Firestore succeeds, and
the upload to the other service

00:12:34.540 --> 00:12:37.720
fails, and the
function is retried,

00:12:37.720 --> 00:12:39.940
another document with
the same contents

00:12:39.940 --> 00:12:42.220
will be added to the
Firestore, so we'll

00:12:42.220 --> 00:12:48.070
have a duplicate which can
also be seen in the Firestore

00:12:48.070 --> 00:12:50.640
database viewer.

00:12:50.640 --> 00:12:53.460
But this may also happen
if the Firestore was

00:12:53.460 --> 00:12:57.390
replaced with another system.

00:12:57.390 --> 00:13:01.500
And as we know, we
don't want duplicates.

00:13:01.500 --> 00:13:03.870
They may introduce
further inconsistencies.

00:13:03.870 --> 00:13:06.660
They may cause
accounting problems.

00:13:06.660 --> 00:13:08.610
The users may be confused.

00:13:08.610 --> 00:13:10.980
They are not good.

00:13:10.980 --> 00:13:15.940
To solve this problem
we'll use event ID.

00:13:15.940 --> 00:13:20.850
This is a number which
uniquely identifies each event,

00:13:20.850 --> 00:13:24.870
and what is important here
is that even if the function

00:13:24.870 --> 00:13:27.820
execution is retried
for the same event,

00:13:27.820 --> 00:13:30.660
the event ID itself
remains unchanged.

00:13:33.200 --> 00:13:35.390
How do we use it in our code?

00:13:35.390 --> 00:13:41.810
We first extract the event
ID from the event context,

00:13:41.810 --> 00:13:47.000
and then we use it as the
Firestore document ID.

00:13:47.000 --> 00:13:51.140
This way if the
function is retried,

00:13:51.140 --> 00:13:53.900
it will not add a
duplicate object

00:13:53.900 --> 00:13:56.540
to Firestore-- a
duplicate document.

00:13:56.540 --> 00:13:59.450
Instead it will overwrite
the existing document

00:13:59.450 --> 00:14:02.950
which exists under the same
event ID with the same contents

00:14:02.950 --> 00:14:05.310
anyway.

00:14:05.310 --> 00:14:08.550
Similarly some other
systems also support

00:14:08.550 --> 00:14:11.420
so-called idempotency keys.

00:14:11.420 --> 00:14:15.300
And if the system that
you use supports them,

00:14:15.300 --> 00:14:20.200
you can use event ID
as the idempotency key.

00:14:20.200 --> 00:14:25.260
As a result we no longer observe
duplicates in the Firestore,

00:14:25.260 --> 00:14:27.810
and similarly we will
not observe duplicate

00:14:27.810 --> 00:14:30.390
in other systems to
which we pass the event

00:14:30.390 --> 00:14:32.620
ID as the idempotency key.

00:14:36.260 --> 00:14:39.190
But what if the system
on which we depend

00:14:39.190 --> 00:14:42.430
does not support idempotency?

00:14:42.430 --> 00:14:44.140
Here's an example.

00:14:44.140 --> 00:14:47.590
This is a function which uses
SendGrid, the email delivery

00:14:47.590 --> 00:14:51.440
service, to send an
email from the function.

00:14:51.440 --> 00:14:56.270
The problem is that the
send call is not idempotent.

00:14:56.270 --> 00:14:57.440
What can we do about it?

00:14:59.980 --> 00:15:01.740
We can record the event IDs.

00:15:01.740 --> 00:15:04.500
This is the general
approach here.

00:15:04.500 --> 00:15:08.740
We use a database-- like
Firestore in this example,

00:15:08.740 --> 00:15:11.610
but other database
could work as well--

00:15:11.610 --> 00:15:14.190
to store the
already-handled event IDs.

00:15:14.190 --> 00:15:17.180
And then when the requests
come, before we enter

00:15:17.180 --> 00:15:19.320
the non-idempotent
section of the code--

00:15:19.320 --> 00:15:23.030
so the one which sends an
email, and if it was retried,

00:15:23.030 --> 00:15:26.580
it could result in
duplicate emails being sent.

00:15:26.580 --> 00:15:31.110
In this section, we first
check by querying Firestore

00:15:31.110 --> 00:15:33.610
whether the event was handled.

00:15:33.610 --> 00:15:38.160
And if it was not, we execute
the non-idempotent section

00:15:38.160 --> 00:15:42.860
and then store the event ID
as handles in the Firestore

00:15:42.860 --> 00:15:44.460
database.

00:15:44.460 --> 00:15:47.220
This way the chance
of unwanted retried

00:15:47.220 --> 00:15:51.240
calls to the other service of
the duplicate emails being sent

00:15:51.240 --> 00:15:52.750
are greatly reduced.

00:15:52.750 --> 00:15:54.480
However, there is
still a small chance

00:15:54.480 --> 00:16:00.570
that if the function attempts
are executed concurrently,

00:16:00.570 --> 00:16:06.210
a duplicate email
may still be sent.

00:16:06.210 --> 00:16:10.950
To practically
resolve this issue

00:16:10.950 --> 00:16:14.640
we can use the lease mechanism.

00:16:14.640 --> 00:16:17.190
It allows us to get
an exclusive access

00:16:17.190 --> 00:16:19.950
to the non-idempotent
section of the code

00:16:19.950 --> 00:16:23.380
for the specific amount of time.

00:16:23.380 --> 00:16:25.840
In this example,
the first attempt

00:16:25.840 --> 00:16:29.020
gets the lease before it
enters the critical section--

00:16:29.020 --> 00:16:32.780
the one that sends the email.

00:16:32.780 --> 00:16:36.080
The second attempt, which
happens while the lease is

00:16:36.080 --> 00:16:39.640
taken, is actually rejected
because it cannot get

00:16:39.640 --> 00:16:41.050
the lease.

00:16:41.050 --> 00:16:43.480
And finally, the
third attempt, which

00:16:43.480 --> 00:16:48.040
happens after the
first attempt failed,

00:16:48.040 --> 00:16:52.030
takes the lease again,
and eventually succeeds

00:16:52.030 --> 00:16:56.800
with handling the event.

00:16:56.800 --> 00:17:01.340
How do we apply this
approach to our code?

00:17:01.340 --> 00:17:04.490
We use a Firestore transaction.

00:17:04.490 --> 00:17:08.540
And this time it not only
checks whether a given event has

00:17:08.540 --> 00:17:15.290
been handled, but it also saves
the time stamp until which

00:17:15.290 --> 00:17:17.810
the current function
execution has exclusive access

00:17:17.810 --> 00:17:19.050
to that critical section--

00:17:19.050 --> 00:17:21.740
again, the one which
sends the email.

00:17:21.740 --> 00:17:25.760
If another request comes
before this time stamp--

00:17:25.760 --> 00:17:29.250
I mean, another retry
of the same function--

00:17:29.250 --> 00:17:30.690
it will be rejected.

00:17:30.690 --> 00:17:34.610
So no concurrent code
execution will happen.

00:17:34.610 --> 00:17:37.340
And with this approach
applied, we practically

00:17:37.340 --> 00:17:39.295
eliminated the unwanted retries.

00:17:42.770 --> 00:17:44.750
So to summarize, there
are multiple ways

00:17:44.750 --> 00:17:47.120
to make your
function idempotent.

00:17:47.120 --> 00:17:51.140
You can ensure that your
mutations can happen

00:17:51.140 --> 00:17:54.050
more than once without
changing the outcome,

00:17:54.050 --> 00:17:58.590
you can record which event IDs
have already been processed,

00:17:58.590 --> 00:18:01.700
you can query database
states in the transaction

00:18:01.700 --> 00:18:06.940
before mutating the state, you
can also supply idempotent keys

00:18:06.940 --> 00:18:10.810
when calling APIs
which support them,

00:18:10.810 --> 00:18:14.020
and finally you can also clean
up duplicates out of band

00:18:14.020 --> 00:18:18.040
through an offline process,
if that approach is

00:18:18.040 --> 00:18:19.970
fine in your workflow.

00:18:23.060 --> 00:18:26.600
All right, so to see retrace
and idempotency in action,

00:18:26.600 --> 00:18:29.890
I will demonstrate a
simple order processing

00:18:29.890 --> 00:18:34.930
system for my fake
restaurant, called Kale Pizza.

00:18:34.930 --> 00:18:36.730
How does it look like?

00:18:36.730 --> 00:18:41.000
Going from the left
side of the diagram,

00:18:41.000 --> 00:18:44.820
there's a website through which
customers can create orders.

00:18:44.820 --> 00:18:47.420
The website invokes
one Cloud Function,

00:18:47.420 --> 00:18:52.690
which publishes a message
to Cloud Pub/Sub topic.

00:18:52.690 --> 00:18:55.390
Then there is another
function, which

00:18:55.390 --> 00:18:58.170
is triggered on messages
from this topic,

00:18:58.170 --> 00:19:00.110
and it does three things.

00:19:00.110 --> 00:19:02.860
First it calls another
service represented

00:19:02.860 --> 00:19:06.880
by another function, which
chooses the cook who's going

00:19:06.880 --> 00:19:10.160
to handle the given order.

00:19:10.160 --> 00:19:13.610
Then it stores the order
together with the selected cook

00:19:13.610 --> 00:19:17.260
in the Firestore database.

00:19:17.260 --> 00:19:21.050
And then it calls yet another
service, yet another function,

00:19:21.050 --> 00:19:23.530
which triggers meal preparation.

00:19:23.530 --> 00:19:26.290
And finally, the
restaurant has a website

00:19:26.290 --> 00:19:30.490
which simply syncs with
the Firestore database

00:19:30.490 --> 00:19:34.570
to view the incoming orders.

00:19:34.570 --> 00:19:36.250
All right, so let's
see this in action.

00:19:43.860 --> 00:19:47.965
OK, so I will click
start right away.

00:19:47.965 --> 00:19:49.860
And here we have
these two websites.

00:19:49.860 --> 00:19:53.920
So one is the client
website, and the other one

00:19:53.920 --> 00:19:56.680
is the restaurant website.

00:19:56.680 --> 00:19:58.920
So the one on the left
generates the event.

00:19:58.920 --> 00:20:00.910
I'm generating them
every second here

00:20:00.910 --> 00:20:05.650
to see more of these
events going out

00:20:05.650 --> 00:20:08.570
to make it more interesting.

00:20:08.570 --> 00:20:12.380
And let's see what we get here.

00:20:12.380 --> 00:20:13.420
So far, so good.

00:20:17.750 --> 00:20:19.520
All the retries
are going through.

00:20:26.540 --> 00:20:29.330
OK, let's wait a bit more.

00:20:29.330 --> 00:20:31.460
All right, let's stop it.

00:20:31.460 --> 00:20:33.680
So what do we see now?

00:20:33.680 --> 00:20:38.480
44 orders went out, and
only 41 orders came in.

00:20:38.480 --> 00:20:42.290
So some orders got
lost here, and I

00:20:42.290 --> 00:20:47.930
can see that, for example,
the first one here, right,

00:20:47.930 --> 00:20:52.090
it didn't make it
to the restaurant.

00:20:52.090 --> 00:20:54.320
It didn't make it through
the whole pipeline.

00:20:54.320 --> 00:20:56.660
So something is
wrong here, and lost

00:20:56.660 --> 00:21:00.900
orders means unhappy customers--
not good for your business.

00:21:00.900 --> 00:21:03.090
So let's investigate
what happened.

00:21:03.090 --> 00:21:08.450
So I have the functions
I've deployed in my project,

00:21:08.450 --> 00:21:12.140
and the main function,
the one which

00:21:12.140 --> 00:21:17.570
performs those three actions,
is the process order function.

00:21:17.570 --> 00:21:21.530
And like I show in the
diagram, it does three things.

00:21:21.530 --> 00:21:23.870
It calls the choose
cook service,

00:21:23.870 --> 00:21:27.800
then it stores the
order in the database,

00:21:27.800 --> 00:21:30.230
and then it calls
yet another service.

00:21:30.230 --> 00:21:33.950
So probably something
failed here.

00:21:33.950 --> 00:21:38.600
So let's see, and here is
where the observability tools

00:21:38.600 --> 00:21:44.630
in Google Cloud
Platform are useful.

00:21:44.630 --> 00:21:50.000
First, I can see that
on the executions chart,

00:21:50.000 --> 00:21:51.810
there are some errors, indeed.

00:21:51.810 --> 00:21:56.290
Most requests were OK, but
there are also some errors.

00:21:56.290 --> 00:22:01.040
And if I scroll down, I can see
the errors from the last seven

00:22:01.040 --> 00:22:06.600
days, and if I click here I go
to Stackdriver Error Reporting,

00:22:06.600 --> 00:22:10.230
in which I can see
the errors chart.

00:22:10.230 --> 00:22:12.980
I can see a simple stack trace.

00:22:12.980 --> 00:22:17.640
And finally, I
can see some logs.

00:22:17.640 --> 00:22:21.950
OK, let's go to this one--

00:22:21.950 --> 00:22:24.240
or this one.

00:22:24.240 --> 00:22:30.960
I can click View Logs,
and see the logs here.

00:22:30.960 --> 00:22:35.270
All right, so if I filter
only by this execution ID,

00:22:35.270 --> 00:22:39.060
I can see a simple
function invocation.

00:22:39.060 --> 00:22:41.430
So the function execution
started, received an order,

00:22:41.430 --> 00:22:43.670
but then it got
the transit failure

00:22:43.670 --> 00:22:45.950
from the choose cook service.

00:22:45.950 --> 00:22:47.720
And if we look at
the implementation

00:22:47.720 --> 00:22:49.940
of the choose cook
function, we can

00:22:49.940 --> 00:22:52.040
see that it's
implemented in a way that

00:22:52.040 --> 00:22:54.530
only succeeds 90% of the time.

00:22:54.530 --> 00:22:57.350
For the remaining 10%, it fails.

00:22:57.350 --> 00:23:00.260
Of course, this is a synthetic
example of a failing service.

00:23:00.260 --> 00:23:04.160
But as I said, you should assume
that the function that you're

00:23:04.160 --> 00:23:07.130
calling or the service
that you're calling

00:23:07.130 --> 00:23:09.890
may fail for many reasons.

00:23:09.890 --> 00:23:12.920
And similar story about the
other function, prepare a meal,

00:23:12.920 --> 00:23:17.700
it also has the success
ratio of 90% only.

00:23:17.700 --> 00:23:19.550
All right, so we
know what happened,

00:23:19.550 --> 00:23:22.470
and we know how to handle
transit issues, right.

00:23:22.470 --> 00:23:24.080
We can apply retries.

00:23:24.080 --> 00:23:26.420
So I have the second
version of this function

00:23:26.420 --> 00:23:30.380
here, process order retry,
with the same source code.

00:23:30.380 --> 00:23:35.240
It does the same thing,
but it has the retry

00:23:35.240 --> 00:23:38.660
on failure option enabled.

00:23:38.660 --> 00:23:41.910
I use this option when
deploying the function.

00:23:41.910 --> 00:23:45.560
So let's see how this one works.

00:23:45.560 --> 00:23:49.970
Let's switch to the topic Cloud
Pub/Sub topic and Firestore

00:23:49.970 --> 00:23:54.680
collection, which
actually are operated

00:23:54.680 --> 00:23:58.520
on by this second
version of the function.

00:23:58.520 --> 00:24:01.760
All right, so I've got orders
going out, orders coming in.

00:24:01.760 --> 00:24:05.960
By the way, the color are
just color-encoded event IDs

00:24:05.960 --> 00:24:09.320
so that we can visually
compare the right and left side

00:24:09.320 --> 00:24:13.040
and see the differences.

00:24:13.040 --> 00:24:15.210
OK, I can stop.

00:24:15.210 --> 00:24:16.860
And what do I see?

00:24:16.860 --> 00:24:21.050
26 orders went out, and
30 orders came then, so--

00:24:21.050 --> 00:24:22.310
OK, 31.

00:24:22.310 --> 00:24:26.320
31 orders came in, so
definitely more than I sent.

00:24:26.320 --> 00:24:28.070
So there must be
some duplicates,

00:24:28.070 --> 00:24:32.390
and I can already
see some here which

00:24:32.390 --> 00:24:36.920
appears even three times-- the
same order, the same event IDs,

00:24:36.920 --> 00:24:38.430
with three different
cooks assigned.

00:24:38.430 --> 00:24:41.180
So they may start preparing
the meal right away.

00:24:41.180 --> 00:24:44.090
While the customer may be
happy with the extra two meals,

00:24:44.090 --> 00:24:47.460
but still that's not
good for your business.

00:24:47.460 --> 00:24:50.750
So what can we do to
avoid these duplicates?

00:24:50.750 --> 00:24:54.470
And the answer is
introduce idempotency.

00:24:54.470 --> 00:24:56.360
So I have the third
version of the function

00:24:56.360 --> 00:25:01.880
here, and this time I
modified the source code.

00:25:01.880 --> 00:25:07.220
And the piece that I modified
is the one which runs--

00:25:07.220 --> 00:25:10.280
which stores the
order Firestore.

00:25:10.280 --> 00:25:15.050
So I introduce a transaction
which first checks

00:25:15.050 --> 00:25:18.470
whether the given event ID--

00:25:18.470 --> 00:25:23.690
whether the document
with the given event ID,

00:25:23.690 --> 00:25:28.310
or in this case the order
ID, was already processed.

00:25:28.310 --> 00:25:37.370
And I only add it to Firestore
if it does not exist yet.

00:25:37.370 --> 00:25:39.170
This way I also
prevent the issue

00:25:39.170 --> 00:25:43.040
of overriding the same
order with a different crook

00:25:43.040 --> 00:25:44.810
assigned.

00:25:44.810 --> 00:25:47.900
So let's see how this one works.

00:25:47.900 --> 00:25:50.740
So I have the
third version here.

00:25:50.740 --> 00:25:52.540
Let's start
generating the event.

00:25:55.320 --> 00:25:59.890
All right, it takes some
time to process them.

00:25:59.890 --> 00:26:01.750
We should start
seeing them here.

00:26:01.750 --> 00:26:04.540
All right, they're coming in.

00:26:04.540 --> 00:26:13.380
Let's wait a bit more to see
if we get the desired results.

00:26:13.380 --> 00:26:15.720
So far, so good.

00:26:15.720 --> 00:26:18.140
Orders catching up.

00:26:18.140 --> 00:26:20.260
Yep.

00:26:20.260 --> 00:26:24.180
OK, so 29 orders generated,
29 orders came in.

00:26:24.180 --> 00:26:27.680
So no lost orders
and no duplicates.

00:26:27.680 --> 00:26:31.560
And I'd like to highlight that
I didn't touch the services that

00:26:31.560 --> 00:26:34.530
were flaky, that failed
for 10% of the time,

00:26:34.530 --> 00:26:38.880
but instead I reconfigured
the other parts

00:26:38.880 --> 00:26:44.370
of the system to handle this
situation in a good way.

00:26:44.370 --> 00:26:52.830
Moving on, I'd like to summarize
the first part of the talk,

00:26:52.830 --> 00:26:57.220
saying that you should introduce
retries and make your function

00:26:57.220 --> 00:27:05.160
idempotent to improve
reliability of your functions.

00:27:08.730 --> 00:27:11.490
Thank you, and with
this I would like

00:27:11.490 --> 00:27:13.670
to introduce my
colleague, Marek,

00:27:13.670 --> 00:27:16.110
who is going to
cover the second part

00:27:16.110 --> 00:27:19.099
of the talk about
performance optimizations.

00:27:19.099 --> 00:27:20.432
MAREK BISKUP: Thank you, Slawek.

00:27:20.432 --> 00:27:24.050
[APPLAUSE]

00:27:24.050 --> 00:27:25.300
Thank you.

00:27:25.300 --> 00:27:28.980
I'm very happy to
be here with you.

00:27:28.980 --> 00:27:31.760
I wonder how many of you
actually use Cloud Functions

00:27:31.760 --> 00:27:34.800
or have tried Cloud Functions.

00:27:34.800 --> 00:27:37.440
OK, so this is going
to be a little bit--

00:27:37.440 --> 00:27:40.280
it's not such a basic topic,
so I assume that all of you

00:27:40.280 --> 00:27:42.399
know what the cold start is.

00:27:42.399 --> 00:27:44.690
So I assume that all of you
know that the cold start is

00:27:44.690 --> 00:27:47.230
when the infrastructure
brings up

00:27:47.230 --> 00:27:52.096
a new instance that will execute
the cold feature function.

00:27:52.096 --> 00:27:53.720
And you probably know
that this happens

00:27:53.720 --> 00:27:57.080
if there's no instance that
is serving the traffic,

00:27:57.080 --> 00:28:01.760
or maybe that there is a sudden
surge in the number of requests

00:28:01.760 --> 00:28:03.440
that your function
has to handle,

00:28:03.440 --> 00:28:07.010
and then there must be
many instances executing

00:28:07.010 --> 00:28:09.620
at the same time.

00:28:09.620 --> 00:28:12.350
You've probably heard that
functions are stateless,

00:28:12.350 --> 00:28:16.160
and that's because there are
so many instances that came in

00:28:16.160 --> 00:28:18.210
and come out.

00:28:18.210 --> 00:28:23.390
And that's true, but the fact
is that they are not always

00:28:23.390 --> 00:28:27.050
stateless, which means that if
there is an instance that it's

00:28:27.050 --> 00:28:29.000
serving, it's
probably going to be

00:28:29.000 --> 00:28:34.280
re-used for future invocations,
for subsequent calls.

00:28:34.280 --> 00:28:39.350
And this lets you reuse the
state that the instance has

00:28:39.350 --> 00:28:41.240
to improve performance.

00:28:41.240 --> 00:28:45.800
And I will show you a little
bit how you can do that.

00:28:45.800 --> 00:28:49.100
So the first example
is connections.

00:28:49.100 --> 00:28:51.230
Let's assume that
your function has

00:28:51.230 --> 00:28:53.940
to connect to some
external system,

00:28:53.940 --> 00:28:57.050
and you can improve
performance by maintaining

00:28:57.050 --> 00:28:58.670
persistent connections.

00:28:58.670 --> 00:29:01.270
So you establish the connection
at the first function

00:29:01.270 --> 00:29:04.290
invocation in the instance
that it's serving,

00:29:04.290 --> 00:29:07.850
and then subsequent calls that
will hit the same instance

00:29:07.850 --> 00:29:11.700
are going to have the
connection established.

00:29:11.700 --> 00:29:15.410
So this may not be so important
if the system that you connect

00:29:15.410 --> 00:29:18.359
to is in the same data
center or very close,

00:29:18.359 --> 00:29:20.900
but it's very important if the
system that you are connecting

00:29:20.900 --> 00:29:22.864
to is on the other
side of the world,

00:29:22.864 --> 00:29:24.780
and then the connection
establishment actually

00:29:24.780 --> 00:29:26.790
takes time.

00:29:26.790 --> 00:29:28.580
And a small caveat
here is that you

00:29:28.580 --> 00:29:31.185
have to be careful
with the target system

00:29:31.185 --> 00:29:33.710
that you are connecting
to, because it

00:29:33.710 --> 00:29:36.080
may have a limit on the
number of connections

00:29:36.080 --> 00:29:37.790
that it can handle.

00:29:37.790 --> 00:29:39.940
And if you use
persistent connections,

00:29:39.940 --> 00:29:43.040
any instance of the
function that it's actually

00:29:43.040 --> 00:29:46.070
out there serving will
maintain the connections.

00:29:46.070 --> 00:29:51.170
So it may expose the connection
pool of the target system.

00:29:55.080 --> 00:29:57.330
Going farther,
the same principle

00:29:57.330 --> 00:30:00.300
may apply to API objects.

00:30:00.300 --> 00:30:03.810
And such API objects
very often have

00:30:03.810 --> 00:30:06.960
connections inside somewhere.

00:30:06.960 --> 00:30:09.070
So it's very useful to
declare those API objects

00:30:09.070 --> 00:30:13.040
on the global scope in the code
so that the connection is not

00:30:13.040 --> 00:30:15.900
re-established every time
you create the API object.

00:30:18.930 --> 00:30:22.470
Similarly, if there is
a global variable that

00:30:22.470 --> 00:30:26.310
gets recomputed every time
your function is called,

00:30:26.310 --> 00:30:28.380
it's useful to put it
into the global scope

00:30:28.380 --> 00:30:31.050
so that it's completed
once and then reused

00:30:31.050 --> 00:30:34.730
on the subsequent invocations.

00:30:34.730 --> 00:30:37.924
But on the other hand,
it's not always true

00:30:37.924 --> 00:30:39.840
that it's better to
perform those computations

00:30:39.840 --> 00:30:44.360
in the global scope
on the cold start,

00:30:44.360 --> 00:30:47.490
because it may happen that
the value is not even used.

00:30:47.490 --> 00:30:51.440
So if you use the value
under very rare conditions,

00:30:51.440 --> 00:30:55.100
it's better to compute it lazily
and not in the call thread

00:30:55.100 --> 00:30:58.700
because the best optimisation
is when you just remove

00:30:58.700 --> 00:31:01.770
the code that is not used.

00:31:01.770 --> 00:31:04.970
And this is especially
important if your function--

00:31:04.970 --> 00:31:08.600
if you declare several functions
in the same file, which

00:31:08.600 --> 00:31:10.160
you can do.

00:31:10.160 --> 00:31:13.040
And actually many people--

00:31:13.040 --> 00:31:14.690
everyone that uses
Firebase probably

00:31:14.690 --> 00:31:16.610
did that because this
is the common way how

00:31:16.610 --> 00:31:18.910
Firebase functions are written.

00:31:18.910 --> 00:31:23.150
And if you do that, you have to
know that every function runs

00:31:23.150 --> 00:31:25.800
in a separate set of instances.

00:31:25.800 --> 00:31:30.200
So if an instance
handles invocations

00:31:30.200 --> 00:31:32.750
of the first function,
it will never

00:31:32.750 --> 00:31:34.730
be called to execute
the second function,

00:31:34.730 --> 00:31:37.490
because that's a separate
set of instances.

00:31:37.490 --> 00:31:40.340
So if you initialize a
very heavy variable that

00:31:40.340 --> 00:31:44.750
is used in the second
function in the global scope

00:31:44.750 --> 00:31:47.570
it's just a waste of time.

00:31:47.570 --> 00:31:51.920
And that also applies to
those require statements

00:31:51.920 --> 00:31:54.620
in OJS where you
include libraries

00:31:54.620 --> 00:31:56.370
that you are going to use.

00:31:56.370 --> 00:31:59.919
So it's a bit awkward to require
libraries inside the function,

00:31:59.919 --> 00:32:01.460
but if you declare
multiple functions

00:32:01.460 --> 00:32:06.830
in the same file, that's a
performance optimization.

00:32:06.830 --> 00:32:08.880
When you apply these
kind of optimizations,

00:32:08.880 --> 00:32:12.170
please be mindful that
the function runs only

00:32:12.170 --> 00:32:13.910
until the callback is called.

00:32:13.910 --> 00:32:18.500
If you try to execute
something after it's called,

00:32:18.500 --> 00:32:20.810
there will be no CPU
actually to do that,

00:32:20.810 --> 00:32:22.580
and the code will not progress.

00:32:22.580 --> 00:32:27.230
So it may be tempting to
write the code like this,

00:32:27.230 --> 00:32:29.450
to send the result to the
customer-- to the user,

00:32:29.450 --> 00:32:32.360
and then do some
computations to optimize

00:32:32.360 --> 00:32:35.562
for subsequent invocations,
but don't do that.

00:32:35.562 --> 00:32:36.270
It will not work.

00:32:40.270 --> 00:32:46.720
I've told you about caching some
computations inside functions

00:32:46.720 --> 00:32:49.570
in the global scope,
inside an OJS process,

00:32:49.570 --> 00:32:51.850
but you can use also
a local file system

00:32:51.850 --> 00:32:53.390
where you can write files.

00:32:53.390 --> 00:32:56.080
And those files will survive
on this particular instance

00:32:56.080 --> 00:33:01.450
as long as the instance
is alive and is serving.

00:33:04.400 --> 00:33:07.340
But you have to be careful
here because the file

00:33:07.340 --> 00:33:09.260
system is in memory.

00:33:09.260 --> 00:33:12.770
So it's not infinite, and
every file that you write

00:33:12.770 --> 00:33:17.600
is going to consume the runtime
memory of your function.

00:33:17.600 --> 00:33:19.905
So eventually if
on every invocation

00:33:19.905 --> 00:33:23.460
you create a new file, and
you forget to delete them,

00:33:23.460 --> 00:33:26.750
you will run out of memory.

00:33:26.750 --> 00:33:29.600
And when you run out of
memory your instance will die,

00:33:29.600 --> 00:33:33.920
and this means that there
will be a cold start

00:33:33.920 --> 00:33:37.530
for future invocations.

00:33:37.530 --> 00:33:39.780
And of course, when
there is a cold start,

00:33:39.780 --> 00:33:41.550
you lose the state
that you carefully

00:33:41.550 --> 00:33:44.160
prepared inside this
instance to optimize

00:33:44.160 --> 00:33:48.120
for subsequent invocations,
so it's better to avoid them.

00:33:48.120 --> 00:33:51.240
And you can avoid
them by taking care

00:33:51.240 --> 00:33:54.330
of not exceeding the
allocated memory,

00:33:54.330 --> 00:33:56.890
not throwing any
uncalled exceptions.

00:33:56.890 --> 00:34:01.440
Just catch those exceptions,
because sometimes

00:34:01.440 --> 00:34:03.840
the process that executes
the code may actually crash

00:34:03.840 --> 00:34:05.760
that also causes a cold start.

00:34:05.760 --> 00:34:07.260
And when your
function times out,

00:34:07.260 --> 00:34:14.070
also that instance is put down
and another one is started.

00:34:14.070 --> 00:34:17.250
This all was about
caching computations

00:34:17.250 --> 00:34:19.040
inside the instance.

00:34:19.040 --> 00:34:20.280
That's executes your code.

00:34:20.280 --> 00:34:23.510
And this cache, this
state, is available only

00:34:23.510 --> 00:34:25.900
for this particular instance.

00:34:25.900 --> 00:34:29.770
So you have to recompute it
in every other instances.

00:34:29.770 --> 00:34:32.639
It's also possible to
go a little bit farther

00:34:32.639 --> 00:34:35.949
to share some state
across all the instances.

00:34:35.949 --> 00:34:40.080
But for that you need to use
some database, for instance,

00:34:40.080 --> 00:34:41.989
a serverless database
like Firestore.

00:34:44.659 --> 00:34:47.540
So the summary for
this part of the talk

00:34:47.540 --> 00:34:50.060
is that you can leverage
the state of instances

00:34:50.060 --> 00:34:52.460
to speed up subsequent
invocations,

00:34:52.460 --> 00:34:56.150
and I'm going to show
you an example how

00:34:56.150 --> 00:35:00.110
I managed to optimize a
little bit of my application

00:35:00.110 --> 00:35:02.960
to just serve faster.

00:35:02.960 --> 00:35:04.820
And the application
that I prepared

00:35:04.820 --> 00:35:10.700
is converting text to images,
so it's applying caption

00:35:10.700 --> 00:35:14.090
on the picture.

00:35:14.090 --> 00:35:16.700
And the parameters for the
functions are just two--

00:35:16.700 --> 00:35:19.310
the caption to be written and
whether we want some background

00:35:19.310 --> 00:35:20.210
or not.

00:35:20.210 --> 00:35:23.357
And we have very awkward
users that most often they

00:35:23.357 --> 00:35:24.440
don't want any background.

00:35:24.440 --> 00:35:27.230
They just prefer white picture.

00:35:27.230 --> 00:35:31.026
And we are going to
optimize for this use case.

00:35:31.026 --> 00:35:32.150
So what the function does--

00:35:32.150 --> 00:35:35.240
first, it fetches the
background from Cloud Storage,

00:35:35.240 --> 00:35:38.600
then it applies the
caption using ImageMagick,

00:35:38.600 --> 00:35:44.270
and finally it uploads the
result also to Cloud Storage.

00:35:44.270 --> 00:35:47.570
The code is simple, exactly
as you would probably imagine.

00:35:47.570 --> 00:35:52.100
So create a temporary directory,
download the background file,

00:35:52.100 --> 00:35:55.820
load the file into
memory, draw the caption,

00:35:55.820 --> 00:35:59.590
write the file to disk, and
upload it to Cloud Storage.

00:35:59.590 --> 00:36:02.600
Very simple, right?

00:36:02.600 --> 00:36:05.990
And when we debug our
distributed application,

00:36:05.990 --> 00:36:09.350
where we analyze what happens
in such an application,

00:36:09.350 --> 00:36:10.990
it's very--

00:36:10.990 --> 00:36:13.520
we just have to use
the platform tools

00:36:13.520 --> 00:36:16.590
to observe what's going on.

00:36:16.590 --> 00:36:21.230
So we are going to use Cloud
Logging and Monitoring to see

00:36:21.230 --> 00:36:23.180
either logs from
individual invocations

00:36:23.180 --> 00:36:30.750
or plots of some patterns
that are in our system.

00:36:30.750 --> 00:36:32.750
As Slawek already
mentioned error

00:36:32.750 --> 00:36:36.170
reporting, when you can see
arrows grouped by error types,

00:36:36.170 --> 00:36:38.510
and you can directly go to logs.

00:36:38.510 --> 00:36:40.010
And in the context
of Monitoring you

00:36:40.010 --> 00:36:43.520
can also set up others on
certain thresholds of metrics

00:36:43.520 --> 00:36:47.710
and other conditions.

00:36:47.710 --> 00:36:51.430
And so the first thing that we
do is we deploy our function,

00:36:51.430 --> 00:36:52.480
and test it at scale.

00:36:52.480 --> 00:36:56.380
In our case, the scale will
be just 5 QPS for 5 minutes,

00:36:56.380 --> 00:36:59.170
but I think that's
enough for testing.

00:36:59.170 --> 00:37:02.110
And when we start that
we still realize that we

00:37:02.110 --> 00:37:03.760
have quite a lot of errors.

00:37:03.760 --> 00:37:06.010
It's about 5%.

00:37:06.010 --> 00:37:09.550
And when we look at the
plot of memory usage

00:37:09.550 --> 00:37:13.070
we see that the memory
actually goes quite high.

00:37:13.070 --> 00:37:16.500
It reaches the limit of 500--

00:37:16.500 --> 00:37:20.030
of 256 megabytes that we
allocated to the function.

00:37:20.030 --> 00:37:24.070
So probably we
mis-configured our instances.

00:37:24.070 --> 00:37:25.780
We just gave them
too little memory,

00:37:25.780 --> 00:37:29.740
and image processing requires
the memory to load the image.

00:37:29.740 --> 00:37:32.350
So probably we just
need to improve of that.

00:37:32.350 --> 00:37:35.290
But let's first check whether
this is indeed the case--

00:37:35.290 --> 00:37:38.420
that the errors were
because of memory ran out.

00:37:38.420 --> 00:37:42.520
So let's go to the function
page, and let's view logs.

00:37:42.520 --> 00:37:45.070
When you filter the
logs by error type,

00:37:45.070 --> 00:37:49.540
we immediately see that those
errors were memory exhibit

00:37:49.540 --> 00:37:51.550
errors.

00:37:51.550 --> 00:37:54.970
So of course we add more
memory, we redeploy the instance

00:37:54.970 --> 00:37:59.890
with a larger amount of
memory, and we test it again.

00:37:59.890 --> 00:38:01.540
And what happens?

00:38:01.540 --> 00:38:05.060
Luckily the errors
went down to 2%,

00:38:05.060 --> 00:38:06.640
but they are not
completely gone.

00:38:06.640 --> 00:38:08.680
Something's still wrong.

00:38:08.680 --> 00:38:10.810
And we see that the
memory usage actually

00:38:10.810 --> 00:38:13.600
went up in the second test.

00:38:13.600 --> 00:38:16.570
It went up even up to 500
megabytes, which is allocated.

00:38:16.570 --> 00:38:19.360
So it looks like we
have a memory leak.

00:38:19.360 --> 00:38:24.420
And I will let you
look at the code,

00:38:24.420 --> 00:38:30.810
and maybe somebody can find
where the memory leak is.

00:38:30.810 --> 00:38:33.930
Does anybody see the error here?

00:38:33.930 --> 00:38:35.290
OK, one person sees an error.

00:38:35.290 --> 00:38:38.350
Let's see if others see it.

00:38:38.350 --> 00:38:39.720
Second one-- OK, very good.

00:38:45.740 --> 00:38:46.640
OK?

00:38:46.640 --> 00:38:47.330
OK.

00:38:47.330 --> 00:38:49.610
So some of you already
know that the problem

00:38:49.610 --> 00:38:53.750
is that we create
a temporary file,

00:38:53.750 --> 00:38:56.120
and we don't delete
it at the end.

00:38:56.120 --> 00:39:01.850
So the temporary
file is created here.

00:39:01.850 --> 00:39:04.520
We just generate a random
string for the file

00:39:04.520 --> 00:39:06.160
that we are going to generate.

00:39:06.160 --> 00:39:07.910
And it has generated,
we write it to disk,

00:39:07.910 --> 00:39:09.130
and we never delete it.

00:39:09.130 --> 00:39:13.430
So this works as I described
it, the first part,

00:39:13.430 --> 00:39:16.610
but whenever you write something
to your local storage it's

00:39:16.610 --> 00:39:19.400
consuming your memory.

00:39:19.400 --> 00:39:20.690
So it's very easy to fix.

00:39:20.690 --> 00:39:23.080
We actually just need
to delete the file

00:39:23.080 --> 00:39:25.070
before we finish the
function, and that's

00:39:25.070 --> 00:39:27.170
the fix that we applied.

00:39:27.170 --> 00:39:33.420
And with this memory leak
fixed, we immediately see that,

00:39:33.420 --> 00:39:37.070
firstly, we don't
have any 500's--

00:39:37.070 --> 00:39:39.190
no errors, and also
the memory usage

00:39:39.190 --> 00:39:46.490
went down even below this 256
limit that we applied before.

00:39:46.490 --> 00:39:48.590
So the question is--
shall we now reduce

00:39:48.590 --> 00:39:52.380
the amount of memory
for the function or not?

00:39:52.380 --> 00:39:54.290
But let's take a look
at the performance.

00:39:54.290 --> 00:39:57.320
We see that with more
memory, our function actually

00:39:57.320 --> 00:39:59.960
runs faster, and that's
because this is how

00:39:59.960 --> 00:40:03.710
we allocate CPU to functions.

00:40:03.710 --> 00:40:08.420
The more memory the function
has, the more CPU it also gets.

00:40:08.420 --> 00:40:13.940
And giving twice more memory
gives also twice more CPU,

00:40:13.940 --> 00:40:15.470
so your function runs faster.

00:40:15.470 --> 00:40:17.120
And in our case, we
would like to have

00:40:17.120 --> 00:40:21.020
the system quite performance,
so two seconds of latency

00:40:21.020 --> 00:40:24.510
is probably quite good, but
four seconds is a lot too much.

00:40:24.510 --> 00:40:28.310
So let's stick to
this instance size

00:40:28.310 --> 00:40:31.980
and this amount of CPU,
in fact, for their work.

00:40:31.980 --> 00:40:36.470
So now let's take a closer look
where the function actually

00:40:36.470 --> 00:40:41.270
spends time on, what's
the most costly part.

00:40:41.270 --> 00:40:42.380
So we go to Cloud Logging.

00:40:42.380 --> 00:40:45.620
We select an individual
invocation, we click on it,

00:40:45.620 --> 00:40:48.740
and then we can filter logs
from just one invocation.

00:40:48.740 --> 00:40:51.950
And you can see that
the majority of time

00:40:51.950 --> 00:40:55.580
is spent on uploading the
file to Cloud Storage.

00:40:55.580 --> 00:40:56.990
That's quite surprising.

00:40:56.990 --> 00:40:58.340
Google has a good network.

00:40:58.340 --> 00:41:00.950
Why would that be so slow?

00:41:00.950 --> 00:41:08.370
And the fact is that we actually
used very bad compression type.

00:41:08.370 --> 00:41:11.090
And by the way, this
is not a very good idea

00:41:11.090 --> 00:41:13.070
to look at one
individual invocation

00:41:13.070 --> 00:41:17.690
to analyze what's going on in
the distributed application

00:41:17.690 --> 00:41:23.150
because this may be just
some random problems here,

00:41:23.150 --> 00:41:26.142
and maybe in other cases
everything will work fine.

00:41:26.142 --> 00:41:28.100
So it's much better to
report customer metrics.

00:41:28.100 --> 00:41:30.230
Use Stackdriver, for
instance, and look

00:41:30.230 --> 00:41:31.760
at the behavior at scale.

00:41:31.760 --> 00:41:35.220
But in our case logs
gave us this information.

00:41:35.220 --> 00:41:37.250
So the problem with
uploading to Cloud Storage

00:41:37.250 --> 00:41:39.230
was that the files were big.

00:41:39.230 --> 00:41:43.460
We used BMP file images,
which were about 7 megabytes,

00:41:43.460 --> 00:41:45.629
while PNG took
only 10 kilobytes.

00:41:45.629 --> 00:41:47.420
So the first optimization
that we can apply

00:41:47.420 --> 00:41:51.320
is to switch that
compression from BMP to PNG--

00:41:51.320 --> 00:41:52.520
very simple.

00:41:52.520 --> 00:41:57.290
And by the way, also
this uploading speed

00:41:57.290 --> 00:42:01.130
depends on the instance size,
because uploading data requires

00:42:01.130 --> 00:42:02.280
a little bit of the CPU.

00:42:02.280 --> 00:42:05.930
So you can also improve on
that by giving the instance

00:42:05.930 --> 00:42:08.780
more memory and more CPU.

00:42:08.780 --> 00:42:13.580
So when we change
the compression type,

00:42:13.580 --> 00:42:14.900
our function runs faster--

00:42:14.900 --> 00:42:17.950
almost twice, much better.

00:42:17.950 --> 00:42:20.000
Can we do better?

00:42:20.000 --> 00:42:21.270
Of course.

00:42:21.270 --> 00:42:23.840
I mentioned that our users
are a bit weird, that they

00:42:23.840 --> 00:42:24.800
don't like backgrounds.

00:42:24.800 --> 00:42:26.887
They like black and white.

00:42:26.887 --> 00:42:28.970
So we don't really have
to download the background

00:42:28.970 --> 00:42:30.440
image on every invocation.

00:42:30.440 --> 00:42:31.830
Let's just not do that.

00:42:31.830 --> 00:42:33.420
And this is actually
the case that I

00:42:33.420 --> 00:42:38.180
tested, without any background,
just black and white.

00:42:38.180 --> 00:42:40.070
So we can download
the background the

00:42:40.070 --> 00:42:44.390
when it's needed, and we
can download it only once.

00:42:44.390 --> 00:42:49.380
And with this optimization we
save another 50 milliseconds.

00:42:49.380 --> 00:42:53.420
And some of you may have noticed
that on some previous slides

00:42:53.420 --> 00:42:56.420
it was 90 milliseconds--
or 80 for this phase,

00:42:56.420 --> 00:42:58.970
for downloading the background.

00:42:58.970 --> 00:43:02.330
And this is what I mentioned,
that one individual invocation

00:43:02.330 --> 00:43:05.980
will not give you precise data.

00:43:05.980 --> 00:43:08.150
You need to have
multiple invocations,

00:43:08.150 --> 00:43:12.140
and that's why metrics are
always better than individual

00:43:12.140 --> 00:43:15.710
logs from one invocation.

00:43:15.710 --> 00:43:17.780
So now we can also--

00:43:17.780 --> 00:43:22.610
I mentioned that it's useful to
move computations that can be

00:43:22.610 --> 00:43:24.900
done just once to global scope.

00:43:24.900 --> 00:43:28.340
So in the next step
I move to creating

00:43:28.340 --> 00:43:31.460
of the temporary directory
into the global scope,

00:43:31.460 --> 00:43:34.040
and creating the GCS
bucket object, which

00:43:34.040 --> 00:43:37.940
is this-- we always use the same
bucket for uploading the image,

00:43:37.940 --> 00:43:39.780
and also for downloading
the background.

00:43:39.780 --> 00:43:42.410
So we can you just move those
two lines into the global

00:43:42.410 --> 00:43:45.980
scope, and that will save
you another 25 milliseconds--

00:43:45.980 --> 00:43:50.060
not so much, but still
significant maybe--

00:43:50.060 --> 00:43:51.510
more happy users.

00:43:51.510 --> 00:43:54.530
So to summarize, what
we did here, we fixed--

00:43:54.530 --> 00:43:57.380
firstly we fixed the memory
leak in our application.

00:43:57.380 --> 00:44:00.340
We increased the instance
size to give it more CPU.

00:44:00.340 --> 00:44:02.120
We used better
compression so that we

00:44:02.120 --> 00:44:06.420
don't spend so much CPU
time on uploading images

00:44:06.420 --> 00:44:07.660
to Cloud Storage.

00:44:07.660 --> 00:44:09.920
We lazy downloaded
the background image

00:44:09.920 --> 00:44:12.290
because it wasn't
using all invocations.

00:44:12.290 --> 00:44:14.080
And finally we moved
some computations

00:44:14.080 --> 00:44:17.270
to the global scope so that
it's performed only once.

00:44:17.270 --> 00:44:20.750
And while doing that, we
use the platform tools

00:44:20.750 --> 00:44:22.070
to observe our systems.

00:44:22.070 --> 00:44:26.290
We used logging, we used
monitoring-- maybe we didn't

00:44:26.290 --> 00:44:27.440
use logging the best way.

00:44:27.440 --> 00:44:29.770
We could have used
more monitoring.

00:44:29.770 --> 00:44:34.460
And that's about
that, but we still

00:44:34.460 --> 00:44:37.130
have time to answer
your questions.

00:44:37.130 --> 00:44:41.080
[MUSIC PLAYING]

